    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d390">

                <pb facs="0591" n="491" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg491"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.11W892/2464</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-04-20">April 20, 1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Dr. Leitner<anchor n="95" xml:id="d390-f1"/> called this afternoon to tell me
                    that he was off tomorrow on a short holiday to Germany. I thanked him for his
                    call and said that I would like to avail myself of this opportunity to speak to
                        <pb facs="0592" n="492" xml:id="pg492"/> him about something which had
                    slipped my mind this morning during the Ambassador’s call. I then referred to
                    the information which we had received from Mr. Lohmann<anchor n="96"
                        xml:id="d390-f2"/> to the effect that the German Government agreed to a
                    favorable settlement now of the various claims which had already been approved
                    by the Mixed Claims Commission, with the exception of one claim (the Drier
                    claim), which must be referred to the Commission for reexamination. I said that
                    it was unfortunate, in my opinion, that the German Government was holding out in
                    this one particular case because it might be of interest to him to know that we
                    had received a good many inquiries about it from the White House. I wished very
                    much, therefore, that the matter could be reconsidered and that this claim could
                    be favorably settled together with all the others.</p>
                <p>Dr. Leitner said that he knew little about the subject beyond the fact that
                    instructions from Berlin had been very precise, but nevertheless he would look
                    into it and see what could be done.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d390-f1" n="95" xml:id="d390-f1-content">
                <p>Rudolf Leitner, Counselor of the German Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d390-f2" n="96" xml:id="d390-f2-content">
                <p>Johann G. Lohmann, German Agent on the German-American Mixed Claims
                    Commission.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
